correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422059} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3594b73-6f5e-59d7-af33-eca899d7a69b" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9b592e-793d-59df-bfb8-afd6396e98f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422043} {:answer/id #uuid "c97cf99c-278e-5e62-ad34-c0d39337404c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422103} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f7eed7-0a9e-5b73-9c5e-87459ac27698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422106} {:answer/id #uuid "f6611b2a-14ad-5e0c-89e6-18073c395569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422103} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9f1c5cb-9a71-5305-8195-bf7acc2a7402" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422152}] :question/id #uuid "21379b36-910e-5f85-8d70-2437c1f661cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-332918" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\n Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\n Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166544 :question/id #uuid "986f9190-9e73-5e08-b08d-ec779933b0cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21635" :question/problem "![](images/27/270b7c6ff796937603596f26e70886f41438ce87c043d3a1b4fc221398b8692f.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T,$ respectively. \n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1,$ $\\lambda_2,$ and $0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n![](images/0c/0c4b7d443c58e6720389306e73eb4e5e1934cfb7359221f36fb43ceb3e91e501.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/32/3244f8128366a3f7f4ae3e41cd9323b1712f9587943f9fb2de54ee0c458feedf.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d241239c6475fbcf9559c2fcbbb8ca2805a6692fd1a9b3966fe8b38441075a4d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/c3/c3ac6997c0e8580f8d91b88578a02043ca495e99e3ea67bdf91b3eab9eb43d98.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 >  0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/77/77200c9fefd57e0b82b796072e07411f5fe10c91185d7d8f665f7ef6c3f54360.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 0,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fb40117d63e76b2b2db59313b173b04a9a23ef2a28493a5041584e4c7c60c67d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/0c/0ce3220f2cd013daba6b1f44f88d2834021b44ba877980f5ec47df7bc52396ce.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2.$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c028d5c0-d51b-5666-be8a-0b2124805e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164273} {:answer/id #uuid "f73a7b72-0225-50a6-bd30-ab6f393aa4d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164274} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb94045-0702-5db4-be2a-4cde090ba1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164275} {:answer/id #uuid "685ae6da-17ce-5001-9fbf-ecd6a49b500f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164276}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c38cd8dc-d3e5-5222-826e-3f8