70a-e407-5a08-937d-75df54b7f163" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e4606bd-bdbf-5a40-b2d1-eb4cb9a360e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305912} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdcb352-3ca3-5f67-bf30-1c5d5f6a4a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "away from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306333} {:answer/id #uuid "c102c8f9-e034-516d-9783-e9c6e02c3273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306951} {:answer/id #uuid "f06d6940-61dc-55b5-a142-72ebda876a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "away from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306333} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0c2c19c-3caf-5df3-a645-d91316296dff" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308283}] :question/id #uuid "8b5a36f6-c7fc-5f39-8a18-1389505413c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333503" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5c236defa66a00f12cd338ed0012cf91e4d7c15aa4865fc2b1edf5b0e2ea1da.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/97/97f7eb1fbddcd9d927447978efd4c42e705d6835bf0cc74525d42ded8c35346b.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/edc697ab38ae9a56d46181e0a45982b7045224de0cc3c4a786ddbc515f22196c.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538b6e5d-fd93-5930-ad67-61e5d1d45c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422051} {:answer/id #uuid "8553e79f-346e-52b1-ad80-d5bffb94983f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422075} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0b93c0-ad48-5a0d-b8cd-df81f372ed71" :answer-field/key "field-10" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4831d1c1-efe3-50f5-97d2-051bbb240224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422044} {:answer/id #uuid "d50ee472-921f-5d43-8629-7a82d77c759a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422108} :answer-field/id #uuid "27bb3c8c-d5b4-5c32-878a-2198b808f869" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b76b9a-f3ca-5aef-8dd2-923e977e70e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }