answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529607} :answer-field/id #uuid "d09acdef-394b-56b7-aede-21eb94c695dd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530373}] :question/id #uuid "21116522-1e68-5aeb-8229-3d0039b396bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333649" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=2x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/33/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9108600b-92c8-5cac-8349-d5b082ccbc72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529932} {:answer/id #uuid "ea06fb62-1823-5c87-b12d-1aaa69446262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529932} :answer-field/id #uuid "067d8b08-6895-5be5-a9f6-a2b1e3667c90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cac2988-a080-595f-843f-97eb5958cbbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529602} {:answer/id #uuid "1c231f71-e7d0-538f-b96d-5930e103e800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529636} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b1ce46-65e7-5567-a0a6-4ebda43fe0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530047} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eab285f-c597-546c-aa55-410e9e2e5d6f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530289} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee89a1e-68f4-5ee3-bf52-c052f8a4875b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529645} {:answer/id #uuid "b840c1f0-2b0c-58d2-be26-ac4a951a3ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530040} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2e6217-2c75-5d5b-a56a-86f1ab8b7813" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530301} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e311032-7034-5ca1-84d6-f1d9adb98503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529679} {:answer/id #uuid "8a40f91d-daa6-5f8c-9c8d-0263fa5d7838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529918} {:answer/id #uuid "a393adc3-4827-5198-a81a-abd84742a4b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529985} {:answer/id #uuid "c629958a-0bea-5ec4-9abc-ab8b41352160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529918} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc482d57-60ea-5929-a93c-02bf101b157d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530379}] :question/id #uuid "d325e43f-35f1-5542-8db8-397925f79316" :question/math-academy-id "q-333656" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e5/e5c236defa66a00f12cd338ed0012cf91e4d7c15aa4865fc2b1edf5b0e2ea1da.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3239 :topic/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Real Distinct Eigenvalues"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101683} :activity/id #uuid "ba6b04cd-0e14-5cbc-8d10-bae9558ae0bb" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101961 :tutorial/content "Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0.$ For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3,$ we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 23\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A \\cdot I_3 = A.$ You can check that $I_3 \\cdot A = A$ using the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general: \n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1,$ namely that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\n for any real number $a.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5383efb1-6e51-562a-bf79-9ab8cba87ed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2995 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "880e64ac-57e9-5d82-9ecc-43590af3ef76" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2f5234-df9c-5269-8702-30b4dde9a2ca"} :step/id #uuid "c7473014-2f99-59a1-b6cb-a5a8ad7926aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101962 :tutorial/content "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A.$\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2,$ the result is the second column of $A.$\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c46b879-fe8a-5f8e-8579-71b1bc92ac07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2996 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column of the Identity Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3cad7c8a-e8e1-5ac0-bdff-f4886c348fb7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101686}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee"} :step/id #uuid "2a8c2469-c4d7-571d-a243-2cf1fe09ed32" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101965 :tutorial/content "We've been using the fact that $AI_n = I_n A = A$ for any $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ But how do we know for sure?\n\nLet's prove the case of $n=2.$ That is, let's prove that $AI_2=I_2A = A,$ where $A$ is any $2\\times2$ matrix.\n\nAny general $2 \\times 2$ matrix can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n\nThen, multiplying by $I_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11}\\cdot 1+{a}_{12}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{11}\\cdot 0+{a}_{12}\\cdot 1 \\\\ {a}_{21}\\cdot 1+{a}_{22}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{21}\\cdot 0+{a}_{22}\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above proof shows that the property $A I_2 = I_2 A = A$ holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $A.$\n\nIn general, to prove that the property $AI_n = I_n A = A$ holds for all $n \\times n$ matrices $A,$ we can use the same method." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c2408f2-4c4d-5bc6-bd0c-e649f5e542f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3132 :tutorial/title "Proving Properties Related to the Identity Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "acbf3f49-a158-5e75-b433-69f7dd172d90"}] :activity/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101038}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100696 :question/id #uuid "f7868f7f-b296-5a38-9af6-61dc4d1198a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4408" :question/problem "Calculate the product $I_3 B$ given that $I_3$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix and\n![](images/9b/9b8d8f439845a4949789fe313997d2245249e1a4df58c750d7cda0af36f1ce18.png)" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $I_3$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n![](images/ac/ac591cf90fae6e8275fed52c26c8487bff9d654733d414974631948fe30b8150.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2f5234-df9c-5269-8702-30b4dde9a2ca" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539285 :question-generator/id #uuid "744d4524-65a7-5e50-bc2e-dccc85dd98bd" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186100875.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8561cf7d-5905-51f0-afa8-dfa160c2a666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The product is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098182} {:answer/id #uuid "9d75e31c-6d5f-5ff7-bfb7-9af46b05c952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098183} {:answer/id #uuid "b04ab11c-1e23-5a8a-ace9-0a5b7e812fa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098184} {:answer/id #uuid "d9147d52-f2e7-5bda-9f5b-fe0aae8f7137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098185} {:answer/id #uuid "730c731f-1318-55a0-8381-b58852458731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098186}] :answer-field/id #uuid "eb3b92a3-f788-5f9e-bc32-c27df022209b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3819a4e-2124-51a9-966d-a232f795727b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388506} {:answer/id #uuid "e5497e19-b13b-5352-a486-89ae75f2fe36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388507} {:answer/id #uuid "1524532d-303c-57e8-baaa-e00f625a908a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388508} {:answer/id #uuid "f505e5ac-8344-5745-ab05-6ca6bf802e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The product is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388509} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6230bd-503c-59b4-8461-692f556f4ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388507} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bf67a40-3ab5-50a6-9561-e2ee6d875a8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388505}] :question/id #uuid "612ed9a2-f644-5c99-b797-cab8e5ee69a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-13762" :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{2}B$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}\\cdot B=B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90412f20-4e0b-5b47-9010-888ec3e2cb1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer